  "projects": {
    "2018": [
      {
        "title": "Improve UI protocol/API to facilitate consistent GUIs in embedding applications",
        "short_description": "This project aims to improve the existing UI protocol to let other applications embed Neovim instances to provide \"vim mode\" like features. The...",
        "description": "<p>This project aims to improve the existing UI protocol to let other applications embed <em>Neovim</em> instances to provide \"vim mode\" like features. The improved API will equip the embedders with even finer control of the GUI to provide a more integrated and rich experience. It would also externalize all the individual elements for a finer control.</p>\n",
        "student_name": "Utkarsh Maheshwari",
        "code_url": "https://utkarshme.github.io/blog/progress-at-the-end-of-gsoc-with-neovim",
        "project_url": "https://summerofcode.withgoogle.com/archive/2018/projects/5425846814769152/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Neovim .NET API Client and Visual Studio Extension",
        "short_description": "The Neovim text editor offers a comprehensive API that allows its functionality to be extended by plugins and integrated into other applications. The...",
        "description": "<p>The Neovim text editor offers a comprehensive API that allows its functionality to be extended by plugins and integrated into other applications. The goal of this project is to create an API client library in C# that will allow .NET applications to conveniently use the API. Additionally, an extension for Visual Studio will be created to integrate most of Neovim's functionality into the IDE.</p>\n",
        "student_name": "Brock Mammen",
        "code_url": "https://github.com/neovim/neovim/issues/8337#issuecomment-412862089",
        "project_url": "https://summerofcode.withgoogle.com/archive/2018/projects/5007950254964736/"
      }
    ],
    "2019": [
      {
        "title": "Multiprocessing Feature",
        "short_description": "The job control feature of Neovim introduced great multitasking support that added new capabilities and enhanced important tasks by allowing them to...",
        "description": "<p>The job control feature of Neovim introduced great multitasking support that added new capabilities and enhanced important tasks by allowing them to be done asynchronously (e.g.: online linting, as in <a href=\"https://github.com/dense-analysis/ale\" target=\"_blank\">ALE</a> for instance). This improved the overall user experience and reduced editing latency.</p>\n<p>We aim to add multiprocessing support to VimL, the built-in scripting language of Neovim, by allowing functions to be invoked asynchronously in separate processes and enabling the retrieval of their results through callbacks and by waiting for them on demand in the parent process.\nThis multiprocessing feature will have a noticeable positive impact on the performance of parallelizable computations and tasks.</p>\n<p>This is achieved by introducing the following built-in multiprocessing primitives based on the job control feature:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>call_async({callee}, {args}[, {opts}])</code></li>\n<li><code>call_parallel({callee}, {arglists}[, {opts}])</code></li>\n<li><code>call_wait({ids}[, {timeout}])</code></li>\n<li><code>:&amp;:{cmd}</code> (invokes <code>{cmd}</code> asynchronously; has a custom predefined handler for <code>:[l]vimgrep[add]</code> commands)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Complementing the multiprocessing feature, is the context feature, which is a set of built-in functions to save and restore parts of the current editor state.</p>\n",
        "student_name": "abdelhakeem",
        "code_url": "https://github.com/neovim/neovim/pull/9943",
        "project_url": "https://summerofcode.withgoogle.com/archive/2019/projects/5254863745712128/"
      },
      {
        "title": "TUI (Terminal UI) remote attachment",
        "short_description": "Nvim works as both a server and a client. “Nvim client” can connect to any other “Nvim server” and Nvim GUIs can show the screen of a remote “Nvim...",
        "description": "<p>Nvim works as both a server and a client. “Nvim client” can connect to any other “Nvim server” and Nvim GUIs can show the screen of a remote “Nvim server”. But the built in Nvim TUI cannot show the screen of a remote Nvim server. The goal of this project is to bring this functionality to Neovim.</p>\n<p>The aim of the project can be summarized as: Whenever the user starts Nvim as</p>\n<p><code>nvim --connect {addr}</code></p>\n<p>the Nvim will connect to the Nvim server running at <code>{addr}</code>, where <code>{addr}</code> is either a <code>pipe</code> or a <code>tcp</code> address. The Nvim client instance would send input to the remote Nvim server and reflects the UI of the remote Nvim server. So the Nvim client acts like any other external GUI.</p>\n<p>Thus, this project makes <code>TUI</code> an RPC client. This enables us to convert <code>nvim</code> from a multi-threaded process, where <code>TUI</code> and <code>nvim server</code> ran on separate threads, to a multi-process system with <code>TUI</code> and the <code>server</code> running as separate processes and interacting with each other using the RPC API (<code>MessagePack</code>) of <code>nvim</code>.</p>\n",
        "student_name": "H.L Praveen Raj",
        "code_url": "https://medium.com/@hlpr98/the-final-report-of-gsoc-2019-9438df33d6a?source=friends_link&sk=e3286a1e49561f29d71322160419cece",
        "project_url": "https://summerofcode.withgoogle.com/archive/2019/projects/4799834828046336/"
      }
    ],
    "2020": [
      {
        "title": "Improving autoread and file-change detection",
        "short_description": "This project aims to do the following:\n\nImproving autoread by making neovim automatically prompt for reloading a buffer if it was changed outside the...",
        "description": "<p>This project aims to do the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Improving autoread by making neovim automatically prompt for reloading a buffer if it was changed outside the current editing session. </li>\n<li>Dsplaying diff between the changed file and the buffer while prompting for reload. </li>\n<li>Refactoring old code related to file I/O.</li>\n</ul>\n",
        "student_name": "BK1603",
        "code_url": "https://github.com/neovim/neovim/issues/12458",
        "project_url": "https://summerofcode.withgoogle.com/archive/2020/projects/5088690579177472/"
      }
    ],
    "2025": []
  },
